WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.980
Hypothetical question for you.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:08.149
Have you ever seen a group of trees going
for a swim?

00:00:08.149 --> 00:00:11.059
Or gazelles that do, uh, this?

00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:14.009
Or a, um, you know.

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:15.780
One of those?

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.150
If you find these images baffling, you’re
not alone.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:25.109
This is Codex Seraphinianus — one of the
most unusual books ever written.

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:30.270
Originally published in 1981, it’s not an
easy book to track down.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:35.800
Written by surreal artist Luigi Serafini,
the book is designed to be completely alien

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.540
to anyone who picks it up.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:43.190
The world of impossibilities within at first
don’t seem to follow any logic.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:48.460
Not only are the images utterly mind-bending,
but it’s written in a made-up, and thoroughly

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:49.801
untranslatable language.

00:00:49.801 --> 00:00:56.590
And yet, the more you read, the more you find
a strange sense of continuity among the images.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:00.750
That’s because Serafini intended this book
to be an Encyclopedia.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:07.070
An encyclopedia of a world… that doesn’t
exist.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:12.810
So, for this entry into the archive, I’m
going to do my best to document the alternate

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:22.170
dimension Serafini created, bearing in mind
the codex is, by design, meant to be untranslatable.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.950
Like a real encyclopedia, the book is divided
into sections.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.530
The first one seems to be on plants in this
made-up world, and they certainly aren’t

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:31.380
any kind we’d recognize.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:37.039
One page displays alien fruits and vegetables,
which seem to be hybrids of more familiar

00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:43.659
produce you might find in a grocery store
— or hybrids of produce and inanimate objects.

00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:46.549
Oh, and watch out for trees.

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:49.170
It seems they tend to run away.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.909
Continuing our psychedelic tour, the next
section is on animals.

00:01:52.909 --> 00:02:00.909
We’ve got some very unusual species of birds,
some fish which look like watchful eyes, and,

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:05.560
uh, this guy: who seems to burrow underground.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:11.370
The codex also goes into microorganisms, tiny
species which are impossible to categorize,

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.360
and seem to emerge from a gigantic rainbow.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.190
We’re just going to have to roll with that
one.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.530
The next section is on legs.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:24.050
Just pairs of legs with peculiar objects emerging
from where the torsos should be.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.700
And it seems there’s a lot of them in this
alternate dimension.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.860
Sometimes the legs walk in the park.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.170
Sometimes the legs walk in the street.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:36.190
Look: I’m not going to pretend I understand
this section, but there’s something about

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.810
it I find strangely hilarious.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.820
The next section is also difficult to categorize,
but seems to dive into the world of chemistry

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.290
and subatomic particles.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:53.030
Within these pages, you can find rows and
rows of molecules which feel so scientific.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:56.870
Of course, like the rest of the book this
is all made up, but there’s something about

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:02.170
going all the way down to the atomic level
that makes this fictional realm feel strangely

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:03.170
real.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:08.920
Next, we have a short section on vehicles,
like this car made out of flies and white

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:09.920
goo.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.940
Or this other vehicle made out… something?

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:18.150
Or this cloud shaped vehicle, which in another
section can be seen making rainbows, although

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.400
it seems to mess them up on occasion.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:26.900
Nearing the end, the next, and by far the
largest section is on the people who inhabit

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.480
this world — or, as close to people as this
world gets.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:36.170
And I will say, the people of this alternate
dimension do seem to know how to throw a party,

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:41.410
whether it be doing… uh… this, or doing…
this?

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:45.050
Honestly, with most of these your guess is
as good as mine.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:49.060
And the final few sections, are just completely
incomprehensible.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:54.370
I feel like Serafini wanted to make sure no
over-thinkers (like me) would ever be able

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.480
to puzzle this world out completely, so he
filled the ending with stuff that would take

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.190
centuries to understand.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:08.370
And with a final page which seems like a back-of-the-book
glossary, we’ve reached the end.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:13.800
So, stepping back from all this madness, what
is Serafini trying to get at with showing

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.019
us this glimpse into another world?

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:20.689
Well, as trippy — and somewhat unsettling
— as some of these images are, there’s

00:04:20.689 --> 00:04:26.530
also a strange sense of beauty to the drawings,
and an enthralling feeling of genuine mystery.

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:30.659
In the end, I think Codex Seraphinianus captures
a feeling.

00:04:30.659 --> 00:04:35.479
The experience of reading it reminds me of
being young and flipping through an encyclopedia,

00:04:35.479 --> 00:04:40.970
staring at the pictures and not comprehending
the words — but feeling a strange untranslatable

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:44.030
world, hovering just outside ones understanding.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:49.280
So, maybe it’s okay that we can’t puzzle
out Serafini’s one-of-a-kind masterwork.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.499
Maybe some things are best if left truly unexplainable.

00:04:52.499 --> 00:04:54.169
Thanks for watching.

00:04:54.169 --> 00:04:59.160
If you liked this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:02.419
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:03.400
See you in the next video.

